// WP21 S2: a `node_modules` **ancestor**, which is the one thing the walk up to
// a package cannot always name (`docs/wp21-packages.md` §10a).
//
// Node steps over a directory that is already called `node_modules` rather than
// searching it, so `node_modules/node_modules/@nish-absent/zed` is a package
// Node never finds. Both compilers do that wherever the module's own name
// spells the ancestor — compiled from the fixture root as
// `node_modules/app/main.ts`, this program is refused by both with
// `` Cannot find package `@nish-absent/zed` ``. Compiled the way it is here,
// `nish main.ts` from `node_modules/app`, the name is `.` and
// the ancestor is `..`: a directory named without being named, which stage1 has
// no `process.cwd()` (WP19 §A3) and no `statSync` to resolve. So neither
// compiler steps over it and both find the package — the same answer from both,
// which is the property that matters, in a tree npm does not produce.
//
// `helper` is private here and private in the package, and they answer
// different numbers, so the exit code is 7 only if each call reached its own
// package's (WP21 S1). The package is under a scope that cannot exist, the way
// `tests/cases/reject_bare_package` spells its own: the walk from the root
// climbs past this checkout, so a name a developer might really have installed
// would make the refusal depend on what is above the repository.
import { zed } from "@nish-absent/zed";

const helper = (): i32 => 2;

export const main = (): i32 => zed() + helper();
